what we did was we went and took  existing data from many many clinical  trials uh we trained a gradient boosting  machine we used xg boost in carrick  terror it's an awesome library uh please  do check it out if you haven't already  used it um and we use that to basically  try and predict using pre-pre treatment  information uh whether someone would  respond to a specific treatment in the  future you can then productionize it you  you know wrap the model in in some kind  of api or some kind of binary uh and  implement it in production uh and then  we found that two people who did take a  treatment that was recommended to the  treatment that was predicted to work are  actually two times more likely to  recover with that treatment than if you  know they've taken a treatment that was  selected by the psychiatrist based on  clinical intuition  uh and so there's this you know uh even  when someone gets access to care there  is this clear opportunity for us to make  smarter decisions to make um to make  better use of the treatments that are  available to us uh already  a second example that i want to talk  about is really on the operational side  um again touching on this concept of a  vital sign in mental health and tracking  outcomes so if you're really interested  in this one i suggest another landsat  paper this one was not done by spring it  was done by one of our advisors called  jamie delgadillo based in the uk uh in  the uk they deliver psychological  services through a program called iaps  which is basically a government-backed  program  that standardizes the way that they  deliver mental healthcare and one of the  amazing things that they do is every  time you go in for a session uh before  that session starts you take a short  questionnaire to basically try and  measure you know how many symptoms you  have whether you're whether your  symptoms are good whether your symptoms  are bad uh and then on the back end they  can structure and analyze all of that  data and use it to set clearer  expectations for the provider and for  the patient about treatment about  treatment outcomes about whether things  are going well about things are not  going well they use a couple of our  packages lme4 and ln and lme to do  growth curve modelling on on this data  they take nationally representative data  they can say look when people come in on  average they have a you know a  depression score of 20. uh you know on  the second one it's gone down a little  bit on the third one it's gone down a  little bit and you can kind of model out  that growth curve  and then they can say like let's look at  the you know the deciles here what is  the trajectory of the worst 10 of  outcomes and what is the trajectory of  the best 10 of outcomes let's say okay  this gives us the kind of the bounds of  of what we know is normal if anyone's in  that worst 10 of outcomes at any time  let's tell the clinician let's let's  tell the clinician and let's ask them to  do something about it let's have that  conversation with with the patient say  hey you know usually by now people have  seen this much progress and it seems  like we're only seeing this much  progress you know is it that you don't  like the therapist is it that you want  to try a different style is it that  something has happened in your life  that's made your symptoms worse but you  know to kind of accelerate that  conversation and give the the provider  and the patient an opportunity to course  correct their treatment much sooner  and when this approach was tested in  clinical trials you can see on the  right-hand side these are two um  two km curves uh and basically you see  that the curve for the green line goes  down much steeper than the curve for the  orange line uh which basically tells you  that that the treatment has a two times  lower failure rate people are much less  likely overall to fail treatment or to  not recover for treatment if you don't  do this  so essentially it's another opportunity  where you can use data you can use uh  you know things like data science and  things like ah to do informative  analyses that really enrich that that  patient and provider relationship that  have really important impacts on  treatment outcomes overall  and the third example third and final  example that i want to uh go a little  bit deeper on uh is this issue of pay  for performance um  and so  uh for context in most of healthcare  providers get paid fee for service uh  and that means that you know if you go  and see your doctor every week uh you  you know you pay them whatever 100 bucks  a week every time you go and see them  and what it means is really if you stay  in treatment for longer providers make  more money  and in mental health  we don't even really measure or  demonstrate quality there's no real  concept for quality uh certainly not in  a kind of regular care or treatment as  usual and so what it means is that  providers are really not incentivized to  help you get better faster because  actually you know if you leave you know  if you get better and then you leave um  they now need to go and find a new  patient or they're kind of losing  revenue and so you have this like hugely  misaligned incent problem of hugely  misaligned incentives  and really in an ideal world what we  would like is for providers to help  patients get better as fast as possible  and the providers who can help patients  get better fastest should get paid more  because that means they're doing a  better job right so kind of introducing  this concept of quality and also trying  to move away from a pure  service model uh where incentives are  maybe not necessarily aligned between  the payer and the provider and the  patient  and so the work that i'm going to talk  about uh is from three people uh who all  worked at spring  um the first one is sahar who's a  research scientist at spring she drove  most of this work and so all of the  credit should go to her uh kayla moderos  used to work at spring and they did an  awesome job uh prototyping this out  they've done it in the past in different  areas of healthcare and now they've  moved on to another area of healthcare  working on weight loss and kelsey quick  is a senior director of provider ops at  spring and so um  really brought a ton of domain knowledge  about how can we communicate this  information to providers and how do we  kind of  develop the technology more thoughtfully  and like kind of hand in hand with  providers rather than this being like a  punitive pay for performance scheme  and so basically there's four kind of  components to the to the program  the first is that they developed this  software called candela uh candela  basically goes and pulls from all of the  different systems that we have in the  background uh things like the scheduling  tool things like the electronic health  record uh things like patient  satisfaction all those kind of things  and it kind of develops 12 key kpis  around care so clinical and operational  metrics that that we can agree on with  the provider are important right so for  example what is the wait time the wait  time should be lower uh what is the what  is your average what percentage of your  patients actually improve when they go  into treatment okay we all agree that  that number should you know higher is  better and so we kind of go through you  know these 12 different kpis and we all  are on the same page look these kpis are  really important and we're clearly  agreed on what the targets are for each  of those kpis  once we track all of that we then go and  report all of this information back to  the provider every month so providers  who historically have never had any  information uh about the care that they  deliver kind of flying blind uh you know  generally lacking lacking clear feedback  um now receive a monthly report that  tells them here's how you performed on  all these different performance you know  last month you treated this many  patients and this is how many of them  have made progress and this is how many  of them you know said that you're a 10  out of 10 and they're really happy and  they have a great therapeutic alliance  with you and then we benchmark all of  those data points against all of the  rest of the network so spring has  thousands of providers and so we'll not  just give you your data but we'll help  you understand the context so you know  uh maybe your wait time is two days and  everyone else's wait time is three days  and so therefore you know your wait time  is good  the third part is once they've once  they've kind of burned in and they've  started to receive this uh this feedback  so they know where they stand they start  to become eligible for this bonus  program and so we say hey look this is  where you're at right now in four months  we're going to be paying bonuses based  on these metrics so if you you know do  really well on these metrics and you're  in the top 10 percent of the top 20  you're going to start getting paid a lot  more and if you're in the bottom 20  you know we should do something about  that and so every quarter they get these  cash bonuses to the to the highest  providing  highest performing providers um  and so you know that in itself is  obviously um  motivating for many of the providers and  then the fourth piece which i think in  many ways is even more important is that  we coach the providers we don't just  give them the data in isolation but the  providers receive ongoing actionable  feedback on the ways that they can  improve the care that they're delivering  and this overall will raise the bar for  the for the provided network and  certainly that was the hope um so it's  not just about trying to kind of find  the best providers and pay them the most  and kind of make the super elitist  network it's also about you know what  are the ways that we can coach and kind  of raise the bar of the network overall  by giving people structured feedback and  coaching them to improve over time  i think it's working for two reasons and  the first one is that it really  validates and retains helps us retain  the top providers and so you know  there's uh every quarter they get these  medals depending on how well they do  based on the benchmarks and the network  and and they're really proud because you  know they're getting data and being told  that they're really good at their job  for really specific reasons and so we  get all of these linkedin posts where  people are really happy about it people  send us emails saying thanks and they're  really interested in it  and then the final thing is that it is  actually raising the bar and so  when we go and look at  as providers roll into this program and  they start receiving that feedback we  then continue to track all of the  metrics over time and you see this clear  effect where obviously you know the best  providers i think the best providers are  you know they know what they're doing  already and so uh for the top quartile  of providers the results are pretty flat  suggesting that there's maybe not too  much room for them to continue to  improve when they're already doing a  good job but for all other quartiles if  you look at in each of those different  samples you see a continued improvement  so as the providers start to have eyes  on some of these metrics like this is  what my remission score is this is what  my improvement is this is how long it  usually takes me to help the patient  recover you see sustained improvement  month over month and so we're really  showing that we can raise the bar and  help train these providers to be better  at their job and help help them deliver  better um better results over time  almost all of that was built in r as  well which is pretty wild on the  left-hand side you could just see like a  little example of some of the metrics  that they get and kind of explains it  with percentiles and graphs and tracks  it out over time and then on the on the  internal side we have all of these  dashboards that we can use to try and  help you know um find providers that  might be particularly good at treating  uh particular problems but almost all of  this was built in r so if you are a fan  of r uh pretty much the whole stack uh  uses things like our studio team shout  out to our studio for the team and  connect product connect platform has  been a game changer for us we use a lot  of our markdown and parameterize a lot  of those reports uh obviously shiny and  flex dashboards are also huge and tidy  versus  everyone's bread and butter these days  i'm perfectly on time which is wonderful  uh mental health is a field of  opportunity um i think that you know on  one side it's obviously sad and it's  restrained to see people uh find it hard  to get access to care find it hard to  actually recover and feel hopeless when  they're on that journey  but they're you know the upside of that  or the flip side of that is that there  is a lot of opportunity for us to do  better and so  um you know i encourage anyone if you  know if they're interested in this space  to to try and see what they can do to  help improve mental health care but  there's lots of technology out there  there's lots of data out there that we  can use to help people get better  outcomes there are lots and lots of  places where you can use this i don't  think that it's all about treatment i  think that you can do this kind of stuff  in prevention i think you can do this  thing  this kind of framework in marketing  their mental health is a whole um  a whole landscape that that generally  has lacked data historically and i think  there are many places where you can  bring data where you can structure data  and where you can use it uh  at the point of operations to to make  better decisions and and it's important  to know this is no longer just r d this  is not just happening in academic  settings it's happening every day  happening in the 